{"text": "While discussions of human credulity often portray children as being highly credulous, experiments show that young children already use sophisticated methods for deciding whom to trust. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> In some such experiments, children interact with two individuals who give different names to previously unfamiliar objects. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> The child must decide whom to believe. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> In general, from an early age children would choose to believe a person they have a strong relationship with over a stranger. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> At age three, they would still do this even if the familiar person has a history of having given names to objects that the child recognized to be wrong. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> By age five, though, they will give more credence to a relative stranger who has some history of having been accurate than to a familiar person with a record of inaccuracy. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> What happens if there are two strangers to choose between with similar records of accuracy. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Then the stranger who comes from the same cultural group as the child, as determined by accent, for example, is the one believed. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Experiments have also been done on adults, who, like children, use information about the source of theories to decide whether to adopt them. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> We tend to follow opinion leaders, people in authority and those whose beliefs we share. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Similar experiments show that among chimpanzees, those with higher status are trusted more. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> We all need ways of deciding whom to believe. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Evolution has provided us and other species a start.", "alignment": {"characters": ["W", "h", "i", "l", "e", " ", "d", "i", "s", "c", "u", "s", "s", "i", "o", "n", "s", " ", "o", "f", " ", "h", "u", "m", "a", "n", " ", "c", "r", "e", "d", "u", "l", "i", "t", "y", " ", "o", "f", "t", "e", "n", " ", "p", "o", "r